INT. PEDIATRIC CLINIC - EXAM ROOM - DAY

Bright walls, painted giraffes, a jungle-gym poster. A small examination bed with white paper and a stuffed lion. A scale. Sunlight through a striped blind.

A WOMAN in a white coat, her name tag turned backward, sits on a rolling stool with a tablet: DR. SOLVEIG AASEN-BELLO (40s, kind, tired, mismatched earrings). She keeps her voice level.

Her patient's mother, KAMARIA JOSEPH-FERRIER (30s, nurse's scrubs under her coat, tight shoulders), holds a toddler's hand. The toddler, OSKAR, is asleep on the bed.

And in the corner, on a plastic chair, sits a man, introduced on the form only as THE FATHER (30s, hoodie, unshaven, knee bouncing).

SOLVEIG
He's very warm.

KAMARIA
I know.

SOLVEIG
Has he been drinking?

KAMARIA
Barely.

THE FATHER
He had juice.

KAMARIA
Half a cup.

SOLVEIG
Any rash?

THE FATHER
Not that I saw.

KAMARIA
David, I checked. Twice.

SOLVEIG
(typing)
Fever since?

THE FATHER
Monday.

KAMARIA
Sunday night.

He looks at her. She looks at the child. The pen clicks.

SOLVEIG
Okay. I'm going to listen to his chest.

She lays the stethoscope on the small ribs. Her eyes drift, then fix. Her face doesn't change, but her hand does. It moves up. Back. Down.

KAMARIA
What is it?

SOLVEIG
I'd like a quick bloods and a chest film.

THE FATHER
What is it?

SOLVEIG
I'm not sure yet.

KAMARIA
(very quiet)
Don't do that to me. I'm a nurse.

SOLVEIG
I know.

KAMARIA
What do you hear?

SOLVEIG
A fluid sound. On the right.

Kamaria closes her eyes. The father's knee stops bouncing. He half rises, sits again, and leans forward until his elbows rest on his thighs.

DAVID
(to himself)
I told you Monday.

KAMARIA
You told me nothing.

DAVID
I said we should come in Monday.

KAMARIA
You said wait.

SOLVEIG
Please. Both of you.

She wheels the stool back. Rests the stethoscope across her knees.

SOLVEIG (CONT'D)
He's going to be admitted. Today. He's stable, he's breathing, and I'm going to have him upstairs in twenty minutes.

KAMARIA
Oxygen?

SOLVEIG
Yes.

KAMARIA
Antibiotics?

SOLVEIG
IV. Straight away.

Kamaria nods. Opens her eyes. Squeezes the small hand and doesn't let go. David pulls a tissue from his hoodie pocket. Finds there is nothing in it.

SOLVEIG (CONT'D)
He's going to be all right. It will be loud and frightening, but he's going to be all right.

DAVID
You promise?

SOLVEIG
I won't promise. I'll stay with him.

On the bed, Oskar stirs, opens one eye, and sees the lion. He reaches for it with the whole of his fist.

FADE OUT.
